</div><div class="row r-value"><dt class="i-puzzle">culture</dt>
<dd><a href="https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1044835">culture of the United States</a></dd>
<span class="tools">
        <span class="prov-none" title="—"></span>
        <a class="edit"
       href="https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q30#P2596"
       aria-label="Edit"><svg aria-hidden="true" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20"><path d="m15.4.4 4.2 4.2-3.2 3.2-4.2-4.2zM10.8 5 15 9.2l-8.6 8.6-4.5 1.5-1.2-1.2 1.5-4.5z"/></svg></a></span>
</div></dl><div class="prose-slot" lang="en"><p>The United States is home to <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multiculturalism_in_the_United_States">a wide variety of ethnic groups, traditions, and customs</a>.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-25"
        aria-label="Note 25">25</button></sup><sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-26"
        aria-label="Note 26">26</button></sup> The country has been described as having <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Americanism_(ideology)">the values</a> of <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Individualism">individualism</a> and <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Left-libertarianism">personal autonomy</a>,<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-27"
        aria-label="Note 27">27</button></sup><sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-28"
        aria-label="Note 28">28</button></sup> as well as a strong <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Work_ethic">work ethic</a><sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-29"
        aria-label="Note 29">29</button></sup> and <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Competition">competitiveness</a>.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-30"
        aria-label="Note 30">30</button></sup> Voluntary <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Altruism">altruism</a> toward others also plays a major role;<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-31"
        aria-label="Note 31">31</button></sup><sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-32"
        aria-label="Note 32">32</button></sup><sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-33"
        aria-label="Note 33">33</button></sup> according to a 2016 study by the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charities_Aid_Foundation">Charities Aid Foundation</a>, Americans donated 1.44% of total GDP to charity—the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_countries_by_charitable_donation">highest rate</a> in the world by a large margin.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-34"
        aria-label="Note 34">34</button></sup> Americans have traditionally <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stereotypes_of_Americans">been characterized</a> by a unifying political belief in an &quot;<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Creed">American Creed</a>&quot; that emphasizes <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Consent_of_the_governed">consent of the governed</a>, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liberty">liberty</a>, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Equality_under_the_law">equality under the law</a>, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Democracy">democracy</a>, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_equality">social equality</a>, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Property_rights">property rights</a>, and a preference for <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Limited_government">limited government</a>.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-35"
        aria-label="Note 35">35</button></sup><sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-36"
        aria-label="Note 36">36</button></sup><sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-37"
        aria-label="Note 37">37</button></sup> The U.S. has acquired significant <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hard_power">hard</a> and <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soft_power">soft power</a> through <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foreign_relations_of_the_United_States">its diplomatic influence</a>, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Economic_power">economic power</a>, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Military_alliance">military alliances</a>, and <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cultural_exports">cultural exports</a> such as <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cinema_of_the_United_States">American movies</a>, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Music_of_the_United_States">music</a>, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Video_games_in_the_United_States">video games</a>, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sports_in_the_United_States">sports</a>, and <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_cuisine">food</a>.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-38"
        aria-label="Note 38">38</button></sup><sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-39"
        aria-label="Note 39">39</button></sup> The influence that the United States exerts on other countries through soft power is referred to as <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Americanization">Americanization</a>.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-40"
        aria-label="Note 40">40</button></sup></p><a class="read" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States#Culture_and_society">Culture and society ↗</a></div></section>
            <section class="spread t-columns" id="relations"
             data-spread="relations"
             data-dimension="people"><h2><span class="title">international relations</span></h2><div data-slot="stack">
